**Digital Lead**

Role description for curriculum area Digital Leads  —  Weston College Digital Pedagogy Programme

| **What is a Digital Lead?** |
| --- |

A Digital Lead is a named member of teaching or support staff within a curriculum area who takes a lead role in digital practice for their team. It is not a management grade, not a separate job role, and not an additional qualification requirement. It is a recognition of interest and contribution — and a connection point between the curriculum team and the Digital Pedagogy Coach.

Each curriculum area has one named Digital Lead. In larger areas, a second lead may be identified. The role sits alongside existing responsibilities and is supported throughout by the Digital Pedagogy Coach.

| **The six core responsibilities** |
| --- |

| **1** | **Run the Digital Health Check termly** Use the Teams Digital Health Check to review the area's digital learning environment each term. Share findings with the Head of Area. This is the main data point for area-level digital improvement planning. |
| --- | --- |
| **2** | **Be the first point of contact for digital queries** When colleagues in the team have questions about digital tools, accessible resources, or how to use technology in their teaching, the Digital Lead is the first person they ask — before escalating to the Digital Pedagogy Coach or IT. |
| **3** | **Share good practice** Identify and share what is working well digitally within the team and across the college. This may be through team meetings, the termly Digital Leads network meeting, or a college Teach-Meet. |
| **4** | **Support the Head of Area with digital data in QIPs and Business Plans** Help the HoA articulate digital strengths and areas for development in Quality Improvement Plans and Business Plans. The Digital Pedagogy Coach provides area-level data to support this — the Digital Lead is the link between that data and the area's planning documents. |
| **5** | **Complete the Digital Lead CPD pathway** Work through the three-phase Digital Lead training programme (Digital Foundations, Digital Inclusion, Digital Innovation) at a pace that fits teaching commitments. The Digital Pedagogy Coach delivers and tracks this. |
| **6** | **Attend termly Digital Leads network meetings** Join the cross-college Digital Leads network, which meets each term. This is a peer-learning space, not a reporting meeting — it is where Digital Leads share what is working, hear what other areas are doing, and shape the direction of the programme. |

| **The role is not…** | **What you get** |
| --- | --- |
| IT support or the college helpdesk A management or line management role A formal qualification or graded post Digital Champions — that is a separate, learner-facing initiative planned from January 2027 An additional workload without support | Direct access to the Digital Pedagogy Coach for 1:1 coaching support A structured three-phase CPD pathway (funded, timetabled, college-recognised) Ready-made resources, Health Check tools, and coaching materials A cross-college network of peers doing the same work in different areas Visibility — your contributions are recorded and recognised in governance reviews |

| **How it connects to the quality cycle** |
| --- |

Digital Leads are a named component of the college quality cycle. The Health Check data feeds area QIPs. Digital coaching records contribute to CQRP evidence. Teach-Meet contributions are logged and visible to TLAMs and the VP Quality. Participation in the Digital Leads programme is a measurable output in the Digital Pedagogy Coach's governance reviews at 3, 9, and 18 months.

Digital Leads are not inspected or assessed. They are a quality improvement resource — for their team, their Head of Area, and the college.

| **For TLAMs: next steps** Confirm the named Digital Lead for your area with the Digital Pedagogy Coach — or identify a candidate if one has not yet been agreed. Share this document with your Head of Area and Digital Lead candidate. Contact Graeme Wright (Digital Pedagogy Coach) with any questions or to arrange the first coaching session. |
